// Darwin 1859 — Natural selection. A single passage of about 520 words, not a paper: continuous
// prose, no headings, no numbered steps, and two ellipsis markers that are the transcriber's own.
//
// PROVENANCE WARNING, CARRIED FROM THE SOURCE FILE. The passage was transcribed from the Penguin
// edition and has never been checked against the volume. Neither the wording nor the pinpoint
// p. 168 is verified. Every `source:` below is verified against the transcription and against
// nothing else.
//
// THE SHAPE. Three arguments, serial, the last step of the second branching into two ends:
//
//   <The occurrence of useful variations> — argument from the improbability of a universal
//        negative, carried by an analogy with variation under domestication. Premises linked:
//        take away the struggle for life, or the variability, and the conclusion has nothing
//        left to stand on.
//   <The preservation and inheritance of useful variations> — modus ponens on a conditional
//        Darwin states, in two steps: preservation in the struggle, then transmission.
//   <Divergence of character> — serial: an intermediate conclusion, then the apex.
//
// TWO APEX CLAIMS, because the passage ends by doing two things that do not rest on each other.
// It says what the accumulation of small differences comes to — varieties grading into species
// and genera — and it names the principle. Both rest on the preservation argument.
//
// THE HEDGES ARE THE ARGUMENT. Darwin does not conclude that useful variations occur. He
// concludes that it would be a most extraordinary fact if none ever had, and then conditionalises
// again: But if variations useful to any organic being DO occur. The single place this
// reconstruction detaches that antecedent is marked `interpretation`, because the detaching is
// the reconstruction's move and not his.
//
// THE TWO ELISIONS. An ellipsis marker stands before Therefore, and again before the naming
// sentence. Darwin's Therefore accordingly draws on material this file does not contain. The
// elided text is not restored and not guessed at: <Divergence of character> is marked
// `interpretation` for exactly that reason, and the naming claim carries a note on what the
// demonstrative points back to.
//
// NO OBJECTIONS, AND NO TAGS. The passage is a summary. It reports no rival view, concedes
// nothing to a critic and answers no one; every claim in it is Darwin's own and asserted, which
// is what an untagged claim means. Supplying an objection to give the map some dialectic would
// be inventing one.

